21/04/2026 10:28:16 - Janette Onen (Work notes)
We have been investigating the issue where the Beclometasone/freetext prescription generated extra doses on a 3?day frequency.

Optum provided an audit log indicating that a nurse changed the administration status, which they stated caused the extra dose to be created. They demonstrated the workaround, and we were able to replicate the issue using the same method.

However, when I asked why this supposed administration?status change did not appear on the front?end (View/History), despite appearing in the audit log, Optum were unable to explain the discrepancy. This raised concerns around the accuracy of the audit trail and whether the system may attribute actions to staff that they did not perform.

Following a detailed investigation, I can confirm that the nurse named in the audit log did not change the administration status for the Beclometasone freetext prescription. At the timestamp shown in the audit log, she was changing the administration status for a different patient and different medications.

Testing results:
Administration status updates performed on Patient A (EntityID 756121) resulted in the system creating a new EpisodeOrder for Patient B (EntityID 590972) relating to their Beclometasone prescription.
This behaviour is not consistent with manual user action; the timestamps and note IDs indicate automated or background processes rather than an intentional staff action.
A genuine change of administration status consistently generates the following sequence of six audit log actions:

1.?AttachedNote
2.?GenericPrescription
3.?CancellationNote
4.?NoteCancellation
5.?AttachedNote
6.?EpisodeOrder
This does not match the sequence attributed to the nurse (highlighted in yellow), which further supports that the action recorded in the audit log did not originate from user intervention.

Please see the Nurse Testing tab for full evidence.

Could Optum please carry out further investigation to determine the extent and root cause of this issue? As well as investigating why the discharge is also attributed to this nurse.

