"CS1706064","Berkshire Healthcare NHS Foundation Trust","3 - Medium","17/12/2025 13:34:28","Closed","TEST Enviroment - ADT down","05/01/2026 13:31:47","system","Acute Care Medicines Management Level 1 Team","Azeem Parvez","05/01/2026 12:12:03 - Azeem Parvez (Work notes)
Investigated this further today. 

The error message 'The credentials supplied to the package were not recognized' suggested there is an issue with the certificate. 

i checked the certificate thumbprint for edischarge and took the hash and found that corresponding certificate. The certificate has recently been renewed and had a expired date of 29/10/2026.

When comparing this to the older one which had expired i found that the permissions for the old one had the EIE group on there. (UT02_EIE_SrvAcc) however the new one did not. I also compared this with the TEST PAS as this was currently working. The TEST PAS certificate also had UT02_EIE_SrvAcc added. 

I then stopped the edischarge service on EIE06 and EIE05. I added gave UT02_EIE_SrvAcc permissions on the EIE05 edischarge cert and then started the service back up. I found that this resolved the issue as the edischargeoutbound queue reduced from 126 to 0. 

I then also applied the same settings on EIE06. I checked the application logs and the message regarding ''The credentials supplied to the package were not recognized' no longer appear.

22/12/2025 16:15:22 - Azeem Parvez (Work notes)
tnc 62.192.130.71 -port 10550

ComputerName     : 62.192.130.71
RemoteAddress    : 62.192.130.71
RemotePort       : 10550
InterfaceAlias   : Ethernet 3
SourceAddress    : 100.88.104.73
TcpTestSucceeded : True


Did this on both EIE servers, came back true

22/12/2025 16:05:09 - Azeem Parvez (Work notes)
We are still getting errors on the application log. The following appears when stopping and restarting the the edischarge service :

The credentials supplied to the package were not recognized

A connect request was made on an already connected socket 62.192.130.71:10550

Once the socket has been disconnected, you can only reconnect again asynchronously, and only to a different EndPoint.  BeginConnect must be called on a thread that won&apos;t exit until the operation has been completed.

The first message 'The credentials supplied to the package were not recognized' suggests issues with the certs, in how it has been installed. However the corresponding messages suggest issues with the endpoint. I have cleared sessions stopped the service etc. I have also asked the connection to be 'killed' from their end. I have also checked by running CMD to ensure we only have one connection going out to the endpoint. 

Not sure if the issue originates from our side our theirs.

