09/01/2026 11:10:39 - Sean Prescott (Work notes)
Passing to PA's, Minimum data set below :-

--A clear description of the Problem -

error message every other time using Item Enquiry in 442

(see error.png) attached 

--Time that the issue was first noticed; was anything specific that user or site (maintenance, upgrade etc) was doing at that point? -

CS1707443 - 19/12/2025 13:43:21

--Details of any error messages appearing and their corresponding error log(s) -

(see error.png) attached 

--Look to include screenshots where possible -

(see error.png) attached 

--Workaround offered or provided – Yes

KB0076004

Following investigation we found this occurs due to the SQL Server Availability Group configuration having more than one IP address associated to its hostname, for example when running an NSLOOKUP on the AG FQDN name it returns more than one IP address. The first IP address being the primary server and the second IP being the failover / secondary server.

Development have advised that EPMA does not support this configuration currently and a workaround needs to be implemented in order for EPMA to work without issue.

Our software does not support MultiSubNetFailover.

Can you please have your IT Check this please?

https://docs.citrix.com/en-us/provisioning/2507-1-ltsr/advanced-concepts/configure-sql-server-always-on

--Have you followed all of the steps of the workaround provided on PRB - Yes 

Gave the customer the fix which is the same error as in KB0076004

--Does the current workaround work - No
--If No selected from above state reason why not working and provide screenshots of the errors.

advised customer of follwing

AG is CL7SQLEMIS

FQDN as as shown below

C:\Windows\system32>nslookup CL7SQLEMIS

Server: PICTS003.derriford.phnt.swest.nhs.uk
Address: 10.177.50.3

Name: CL7SQLEMIS.derriford.phnt.swest.nhs.uk
Address: 10.177.51.188

Customer saying this fix is wrong as the 2 ip addresses are correct. stating the following

""Server:""
This line is not a DNS record. It simply tells you which DNS server answered your query. so in this case, PICTS003.derriford.phnt.swest.nhs.uk (a domain controller running DNS)
""Name:""
This is the actual DNS record for the AG listener: CL7SQLEMIS.derriford.phnt.swest.nhs.uk with IP 10.177.51.188

--What Investigation have you carried out / what knowledge based articles have you used - 

KB0076004 also liaised with Adrian Wong who dealt with same issue on CS1433220 for another customer.

--A clear set of recreation steps -

users are reporting error message every other time using Item Enquiry in 442

