This message originated from outside of NHS.net Connect. Please do not click links or open attachments unless you recognise the sender and know the content is safe.
 
select siscode from wproduct group by siscode, locationid_site having count(siscode) > 1
 
The above SQL has returned records. The following SQL will itentify the Products that are duplicated for the stock holding
 
select a.siscode, a.productstockid, a.locationid_site,a.wsupplierprofileid, a.* from wproduct a
join (select siscode, locationid_site from wproduct group by siscode, locationid_site having count(siscode) > 1) b
        on (a.siscode = b.siscode and a.locationid_site = b.locationid_site)
 
From the result of running this you will usually see that the SISCODE is repeated for the same Location BUT the ProductStockID is
different (for the same SISCODE and LocationID_Site). This is the duplicate! You now need to decide which record(s) in the
ProductStock table need to be deleted (or merging and then deleting on surplus record). Check the cost, stocklvl etc to get
a feel for which one(s) can be deleted and confirm with site.
Remove the unwanted ProductStock record from the ProductStock table using the ProductStockID. Backup first.

