05/05/2026 15:15:44 - Sean Prescott (Work notes)
MDS not done as the original issue has not been fixed as per customer

Customer advised the following

Following the closure of the previous ticket, we are re?raising this issue as it remains unresolved and reproducible. While green tick symbols display correctly in Workspace, PDFs generated directly from the Business Continuity (BC) product consistently display green boxes instead of green tick symbols.
 
This occurs regardless of the download method used (Print option or toolbar download) and regardless of how the PDF is opened. When the PDF is opened directly in the Microsoft Edge browser immediately after download from BC, the symbols appear as green boxes, and when the same PDF is opened locally on a PC using Adobe Acrobat Reader, the symbols again appear as green boxes.
 
This confirms the issue is not isolated to Adobe Reader. We acknowledge the explanation provided that the symbols are part of the Segoe UI font and that this font is installed, and we note your suggestion that IT may need to import the font into Adobe; however, if this is required, please advise explicitly how Segoe UI should be imported or embedded, and whether this is an expected dependency for PDFs generated by BC.
 
We also ask how this explanation accounts for the same behaviour occurring when the PDF is viewed in Edge, which uses system fonts. The behaviour we are seeing is based solely on PDFs generated from the BC product directly, and to our knowledge there is currently no PDF viewer that displays green ticks correctly for this output.
 
We therefore request that the issue be revisited and replicated using our BC environment rather than a direct or supplier?side server, as configuration differences may exist. Word documents are not an acceptable workaround due to the risk of unintentional edits and also governance compliance during downtime, and as such this remains an operational and clinical concern that we do not believe can be considered resolved at this time.

+

Also please see below response from our IT team,I feel it will be better if you can attend a call with our IT team,Thank you

I believe this is an incorrect conclusion from Optum support and that they should be able to replicate this problem themselves.
 
The PDF embeds Wingdings as a subset and instructs the renderer to use that font for the check mark. The text layer maps correctly to U+2713 (confirmed via text extraction), but the embedded font subset does not contain the required glyph, which is why all standards-compliant PDF renderers display a missing?glyph rectangle. Because the font is embedded, renderers are prohibited from substituting another font.
This behaviour is deterministic and reproducible across Acrobat Reader, Edge, Chrome, and Poppler, and I can't understand how this could be caused by viewer configuration.

